.TH "WINDOW.CUSTOMELEMENTS" "3JS" "April 6, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Window.customElements \- Window: customElements property
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]customElements\f[B]\f[R] read\-only property of the
\f[CR]Window\f[R] interface returns a reference to the global
\f[CR]CustomElementRegistry\f[R] object, which can be used to register
new custom elements and get information about previously registered
custom elements.
.PP
The global registry is used for registering custom elements by default,
but a shadow root can choose to use a scoped custom element registry in
order to avoid potential clashes in defined element names.
.SH VALUE
A \f[CR]CustomElementRegistry\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
The most common example you\(cqll see of this property being used is to
get access to the \f[CR]CustomElementRegistry.define()\f[R] method to
define and register a new custom element.
.PP
For example:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]let\f[R] customElementRegistry = window.customElements;
customElementRegistry.define(\(dqmy\-custom\-element\(dq, MyCustomElement);
.EE
.PP
Note that the custom element class is commonly defined directly inside
the \f[CR]define()\f[R] call, as shown:
.IP
.EX
customElements.define(
  \(dqelement\-details\(dq,
  \f[B]class\f[R] \f[B]extends\f[R] HTMLElement {
    constructor() {
      \f[B]super\f[R]();
      \f[B]const\f[R] template = document.getElementById(\(dqelement\-details\-template\(dq);
      \f[B]const\f[R] shadowRoot = \f[B]this\f[R].attachShadow({ mode: \(dqopen\(dq }).appendChild(
        document.importNode(template.content, \f[B]true\f[R]),
      );
    }
  },
);
.EE
.PP
See our \c
.UR https://github.com/mdn/web-components-examples/
web\-components\-examples
.UE \c
\ repo for more usage examples.
